\f1\b\fs22 Recognising the start of labour\

\f2\b0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Braxton-Hicks contractions are sometimes mistaken for labour. These \'91false\'92 contractions usually start halfway through the pregnancy and continue all the way through. You may find these contractions visibly harden and lift your pregnant belly.\uc0\u8232 \u8232 It is not known what triggers the onset of labour, but it is thought to be influenced by the hormone oxytocin, which is responsible for causing uterine contractions.\

\f1\b <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Symptoms of going into labour\

\f2\b0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Some of the signs and symptoms of going into labour may include:\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>period-like cramps\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/conditionsandtreatments/Back-pain"}}{\fldrslt \ul backache}}
\f2\b0 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/conditionsandtreatments/diarrhoea"}}{\fldrslt \ul diarrhoea}}
\f2\b0 \
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>a small bloodstained discharge as your cervix thins and the mucus plug drops out (this is called a \'91show\'92)\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>a gush or trickle of water as the membranes break\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>contractions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>First stage of labour\

\f2\b0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The first stage of labour involves the thinning of the cervix and its dilation to around 10 cm. The first stage is made up of three different phases:\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The latent phase
\f2\b0  \'96 Generally, this stage is the longest and the least painful part of labour. The cervix thins out and dilates zero to three centimetres. This may occur over weeks, days or hours and be accompanied by mild contractions. The contractions may be regularly or irregularly spaced, or you might not notice them at all.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The active phase
\f2\b0  \'96 The next phase is marked by strong, painful contractions that tend to occur three or four minutes apart, and last from 30 to 60 seconds. The cervix dilates from 3 to 7\'968 centimetres.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The transition phase
\f2\b0  \'96 During transition, the cervix dilates from 8 to 10 centimetres (that is, fully dilated). These contractions can become more intense, painful and frequent. It may feel as though the contractions are no longer separate, but running into each other. It is not unusual to feel out of control and even a strong urge to go to the toilet as the baby\'92s head moves down the birth canal and pushes against the rectum.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Throughout the first stage of labour, careful monitoring and recording of your wellbeing and that of your baby, and the progress of your labour, is important. This is to ensure that labour is progressing normally and that any problems are recognised early and well communicated.\

\f1\b <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Second stage of labour\

\f2\b0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The second stage of labour is from when your cervix is fully dilated to the time your baby is born. The contractions during this time are regular and spaced apart. As each contraction builds to a peak, you may feel the urge to bear down and push. The sensation of the baby moving through the vagina is described as a stretching or burning, particularly as the baby\'92s head crowns (appears at the vaginal entrance).\uc0\u8232 \u8232 At the time of birth, a doctor or midwife may guide your pushing to enable a gentle, unhurried birth of your baby's head. Sometimes the umbilical cord is wound around the baby's neck. If possible, the doctor or midwife will loosen it, loop it over your baby's head, or clamp and cut it to allow your baby to be born safely.\u8232 \u8232 Once the head has emerged, your midwife or doctor will guide your baby\'92s body so the shoulders come out. The rest of the baby will then follow.\u8232 \u8232 If this is your first baby, the second stage of labour can last up to 1 to 2 hours, particularly if you have had an epidural. If you have had a baby before, this stage is often much quicker.\u8232 \u8232 Monitoring of your condition and that of your baby is increased during the second stage of labour. A long second stage of labour can result in risks for you and your baby. If your labour is not progressing, it is important that the reason is worked out and steps are taken to help you.\

\f1\b <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Third stage of labour\

\f2\b0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After the birth of your baby your uterus gently contracts to loosen and push out the placenta, although you may not be able to feel these contractions. This may occur 5 to 30 minutes after the birth of your baby.\uc0\u8232 \u8232 The muscles of the uterus continue to contract to stop the bleeding. This process is always associated with a moderate blood loss \'96 up to 500 ml.\u8232 \u8232 In this stage of labour, one of the potential problems is excessive {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/healthyliving/pregnancy-bleeding-problems"}}{\fldrslt 
\f1\b \ul bleeding}} (postpartum haemorrhage), which can result in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/conditionsandtreatments/anaemia"}}{\fldrslt 
\f1\b \ul anaemia}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/conditionsandtreatments/fatigue"}}{\fldrslt 
\f1\b \ul fatigue}}. This is why the third stage is carefully supervised.\uc0\u8232 \u8232 There are two approaches to managing the third stage:\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Active management
\f2\b0  \'96 this is the common practice in Australia. After the birth of your baby, the midwife or doctor gives you (with your consent) an injection of oxytocin, clamps and cuts the umbilical cord, and then carefully pulls on the cord to speed up delivery of the placenta. Active management has been found to reduce excessive blood loss and other serious complications.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Expectant management
\f2\b0  \'96 the placenta is allowed to deliver on its own, aided by gravity or nipple stimulation only. In this approach the umbilical cord stays connected to the baby until the cord stops pulsating.
\f0\fs24 <!$Scr_Ps::0>}